Title: "Labeled Expressions"
Id: "98edd056-5d9f-45f0-a8e8-16c63a2a7fcd"

Text: "A labeled formulation attaches one or more labels to a grouped expression, statement, or specification.

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:sum:]
(.x + y.)[:normalized:]
(x = x)(:ref_label:)
(x = x)[:sum:](:ref_label:)
```

Only a grouped formulation may carry trailing label tokens directly, and the wrapper may hold a declaration or specification as well as an expression:

```mlg-fragment
(.x is \real.)[:typed:]
(.*' := `*`.)[:operation:]
(.x is \real.)[:typed:](:reference:)
```

### Multi-Labels

Formulations support multiple labels, either comma-separated within one bracket/parenthesis pair or chained in sequence:

```mlg-fragment
(x + y)[:step1, step2:]
(x + y)[:step1:][:step2:]
(x = x)[:just1, just2:](:ref1, ref2:)
```

### Justification vs. Reference Labels

Mathlingua provides two distinct trailing label families:

- **Justification Labels** (`[:...:]`): Discharged by a matching `[label]` entry in the enclosing item's `Justification:` section. A matched formulation is established via the justification entry's `asserting:` items; an unmatched label is checked inline as usual.
- **Reference Citation Labels** (`(:...:)`): Cite an external resource declared with `. [label]: $resource` in the enclosing item's `References:` section. Every reference label cited by a formulation must match a declared label in `References:`; an unknown reference label produces a compile-time error.

Single-line formulations may also receive a line-level label using `. [label]: ...` under section headings."
Id: "efe841b5-44ed-45f8-8d20-1aacb7fc9bdb"
